     "derivation": "Book Pahlavi has exactly this triple set of spellings for the second Aməša Spənta: Nyberg II lists 'Art-vahišt [ʾrtwhšt], Urt-vahišt [ʾwrtwhšt], Aša-vahišt [ʾšwhšt] — one of the Amahraspands … art, urt < OIr ṛta- … + vahišta- \"the best\"; Aša-vahišt is directly borrowed from Avestan; also the name of the 2nd month of the year and the 3rd day of the month; MPrs ʾrdywh<yšt>; NP ardi- (urdi-)bihišt'; MacKenzie has 'Ardwahišt [ʾrtwhšt' | M ʾrdywhyst, (N urdībihist)] Right, the second Amahraspand' with cross-references 'Ašwahišt = Ardwahišt' and 'Urdwahišt = Ardwahišt'. The adjective is the same word as Sogdian ʾrtʾw: Book Pahlavi ⟨ʾhlwb'⟩, read ahlaw or (with the l/r ambiguity of the script) ahrav, plus the SW doublet ahlāy/ahrāy and the learned Avestanism ašō(g) ⟨ʾšwk'⟩ — Nyberg: 'ašōk [ʾšwk] = ahlav … A learned w., taken over as a religious term from Av. ašava'. The -hl-/-hr- for Avestan -š- is the regular Book Pahlavi treatment of inherited *-rt- via the Eastern-Iranian-mediated religious vocabulary (Nyberg s.v. Mahlāh).",
